@page "/privacy"

<PageTitle>Privacy Policy</PageTitle>

<h1>Privacy Policy</h1>

<p>
    CardiTrack collects health and fitness data to enable anomaly alerts, daily health
    digests, and trend monitoring for the people in your care circle.
</p>

<h2>What we collect</h2>
<p>
    With your permission, CardiTrack reads activity, heart-rate, and sleep data from
    connected health devices, along with the account details you provide during signup.
</p>

<h2>How we use it</h2>
<p>
    Health and fitness data is used only to power the features you signed up for:
    detecting unusual patterns, sending daily digests, and showing longer-term trends.
    We never sell or share your family's health data.
</p>

<h2>How alerting works</h2>
<p>
    CardiTrack compares each person against their own recent history — not a population
    average — and tells caregivers when today's readings sit well outside that picture.
    A human caregiver always decides whether to check in. CardiTrack does not diagnose,
    contact emergency services, or change anyone's care.
</p>
<p>
    For the first weeks we build a picture of a typical day. Until we have about a month
    of data (at least 80% of those days with readings), we do not raise statistical alerts.
    Early “provisional” pictures may colour the dashboard but never page you.
</p>
<p>
    Statistical alerts use the same thresholds for everyone. There is no low, medium, or
    high sensitivity setting yet. A quieter day than usual is a drop of more than 30%
    below that person's average steps; sleep is flagged when it is more than 30% above or
    below their average; resting heart rate is flagged when it sits above their average by
    at least two standard deviations, and never by less than 5 beats per minute; a
    four-week run of at least 5% fewer steps each week is a longer-term trend; and a
    measured zero steps well after the usual waking time (the device is reporting; they
    have not moved) is treated as urgent. A day the device did not measure is not treated
    as a day of no activity.
</p>
<p>
    When a connected device sends minute-by-minute heart rate, we separate the underlying
    trend from ordinary jitter in-process, then a medical language model writes a short
    assessment. The model never computes the numbers; it only interprets them. Only
    assessments labelled as needing attention become alerts. An unreadable answer is stored
    but does not alert.
</p>

<h2>Retention and control</h2>
<p>
    You can disconnect a device at any time, which stops further collection. Contact us
    to request deletion of previously collected data.
</p>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<p>
    Questions about this policy? Reach us at
    <a href="mailto:cloudoperations@codesistance.com">cloudoperations@codesistance.com</a>.
</p>
